Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 10/1/2025
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic faces, fabric, and animal performance; one consistent photoreal medium for all humans and dogs.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE, SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns and tall poplars frame a big house with a wide cool veranda. BUCK (BUCK_ARISTOCRAT: full glossy coat, clean, brass collar, 140 lb proud carriage) strides like a king across gravelled driveways. He plunges into the big cement tank, then escorts the Judge's daughters on a ramble.

(SOUND: splashing water, distant fox-terrier yelps, warm California birdsong)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog strong of muscle and with warm long hair.

Buck carries the Judge's grandsons on his back, rolls them in the grass, then stalks imperiously among the terriers while ignoring the house dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Men groping in the Arctic darkness had found a yellow metal. These men wanted dogs—heavy dogs with strong muscles and furry coats.

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

BUCK lies at JUDGE MILLER's feet before a roaring fire. The Judge reads; Buck's eyes half-close in aristocratic content.

(SOUND: fire crackle, turning pages)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
During the four years since his puppyhood he had lived the life of a sated aristocrat. But hunting had kept down the fat and hardened his muscles.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ORCHARD - NIGHT

MANUEL, gardener's helper, coaxes BUCK through the trees on what the dog imagines is a stroll. A solitary stranger waits at the edge of the dark.

(SOUND: night insects, gravel underfoot, distant train whistle)

MANUEL
(low)
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

The stranger doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar. Money chinks between them.

STRANGER
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck accepts the rope with quiet dignity—until the ends pass into the stranger's hands. He growls. The rope tightens. Buck springs; the man grapples his throat and throws him. Buck's tongue lolls; his great chest pants. Strength ebbs. Eyes glaze.

(SOUND: rope rasp, choked snarls, thrashing)

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

The train is flagged. The two men heave the senseless BUCK into the baggage car.

(SOUND: locomotive shriek, car door slam)

INT. BAGGAGE CAR / EXPRESS CAR - NIGHT TO DAY

BUCK (BUCK_CRATE_RAGE: coat flecked bloody slaver, blood-shot eyes, swollen tongue) wakes jolting. He attacks the man who springs for his throat; jaws close on the hand until senses are choked out again.

(SOUND: train roar, growls, human curse)

Later, in a cagelike crate, Buck neither eats nor drinks for two days and nights. Express messengers tease him—growling, barking, flapping arms. His anger waxes to fever-pitch. At Seattle four men gingerly carry the crate into a small high-walled back yard.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

A stout MAN IN THE RED SWEATER (sagging red sweater) signs the book, brings hatchet and club. He pries the crate open.

(SOUND: hatchet splintering wood, men scrambling onto wall)

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

BUCK launches one hundred and forty pounds of fury. In mid-air the club checks him; teeth clip agonizingly. He charges a dozen times; each time the club smashes him down. Blood flows from nose, mouth, ears. A final shrewd blow knocks him senseless.

(SOUND: club impacts, snarls turning to screams, men cheering from the wall)

Buck's senses return. The man fearlessly pats the head he pounded, brings water and raw meat. Buck drinks and eats from the hand.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten (he knew that); but he was not broken. That club was a revelation. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

Other dogs arrive and pass under the same dominion. Buck watches, learning.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

PERRAULT (little weazened French-Canadian, swarthy) eyes BUCK.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. And seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Money passes. Perrault leads Buck and CURLY (good-natured Newfoundland) away.

INT. NARWHAL SHIP - 'TWEEN-DECKS - DAY

BUCK and CURLY join SPITZ (big snow-white) and DAVE (gloomy, morose). Spitz steals from Buck's food; FRANCOIS (black-faced giant half-breed) lashes the culprit first. Buck recovers the bone and respects the fairness.

(SOUND: ship engines throb, whip crack, dog snarls)

Day and night the propeller pulses. Weather grows colder.

EXT. NARWHAL DECK - DAY

FRANCOIS leashes the dogs onto the cold surface. BUCK's feet sink into white mushy snow. He snorts, sniffs, licks. It bites like fire and is gone. Onlookers laugh. Buck feels ashamed—his first snow.

(SOUND: wind, laughter, soft snow crunch)

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Nightmare confusion. Dogs and men savages under the law of club and fang. CURLY makes friendly advances to a husky. No warning—a leap, metallic clip of teeth, her face ripped open. Thirty huskies close in. She is buried screaming beneath the mass.

(SOUND: snarling frenzy, Curly's agony screams, axe and clubs)

FRANCOIS swings an axe; three men with clubs scatter them. Curly lies limp and lifeless, torn to pieces. SPITZ runs out his scarlet tongue and laughs. BUCK hates him with bitter deathless hatred.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Well, he would see to it that he never went down.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

FRANCOIS fastens harness—straps and buckles—on BUCK (BUCK_HARNESS_TRACE). Dignity hurt, Buck is too wise to rebel. He hauls firewood under Francois's whip, Dave's nips, Spitz's growls. He learns "ho," "mush," swing wide, keep clear of the wheeler.

(SOUND: whip snaps, sled runners, dog pants)

PERRAULT
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

New huskies arrive: BILLEE (good-natured), JOE (sour, malignant eye), SOL-LEKS (long lean gaunt, battle-scarred, one eye). Spitz thrashes Billee; Joe whirls snarling and Spitz backs off. Sol-leks slashes Buck's shoulder for approaching his blind side. Forever after Buck avoids it.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - NIGHT

BUCK tries the tent; PERRAULT and FRANCOIS drive him out with curses and cooking utensils. Cold wind nips his wounded shoulder. He wanders, bristling at savage dogs. Finally he finds BILLEE curled under the snow. Buck digs his own hole, heat fills it, he sleeps.

(SOUND: wind howl, distant dog fights, digging scrapes)

Morning: snow has buried him. Fear of the trap surges—ancestral. He bounds straight up into blinding day.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. TRAIL TO LAKE BENNETT - DAY TO NIGHT

Hard run up the Canon, over Chilcoot Divide, glaciers, snowdrifts. BUCK toils in traces between Dave and Sol-leks. Pound and a half of sun-dried salmon is never enough. He loses fastidiousness, eats fast, then steals a whole chunk of bacon as Pike did. Dub is punished for it.

(SOUND: sled creak, whip, panting, ice crunch)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This first theft marked Buck as fit to survive. It marked the decay of his moral nature, a vain thing and a handicap in the ruthless struggle for existence.

Instincts long dead awake. He learns the wolf snap. On still cold nights he points his nose at a star and howls long and wolflike.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE SHORE - NIGHT

Bleak camp under perpendicular rock. Fire on the ice. SPITZ steals BUCK's snug nest. The beast in Buck roars; they tangle in fury.

(SOUND: snarls, thrashing snow)

PERRAULT's oath and club on a bony frame herald pandemonium. Scores of starving huskies—mere skeletons with blazing eyes—raid the camp. Grub-box capsized. Team-dogs swept back. Buck beset by three; head and shoulders ripped. Spitz treacherously attacks from the side. The men drive the horde off with clubs. Half the grub gone; dogs wounded grievously.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

Wide-open wild water. Six days of exhausting toil at risk of life. PERRAULT breaks through ice bridges, saved by his long pole. Sled breaks through with Dave and Buck; they are half-frozen, coated in ice, run around a fire till singed. Spitz goes through; Buck and Dave strain backward. Dogs hoisted one by one up a cliff by rope.

(SOUND: ice crack and roar, men shouting, teeth chattering)

BUCK's feet soften and freeze. FRANCOIS rubs them half an hour each night and makes four moccasins from his own. One morning Buck refuses to budge without them, four feet waving.

At the Pelly, DOLLY goes mad—long heartbreaking wolf howl—and springs for Buck. He races in terror; Francois axes her down. Exhausted Buck is savaged by SPITZ until Francois's lash gives Spitz the worst whipping yet.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. TRAIL TOWARD DAWSON - DAY

War between BUCK and SPITZ. Buck openly threatens leadership, comes between Spitz and shirkers. Covert mutiny; general insubordination. Pike robs Spitz under Buck's protection. Francois's lash sings but Buck is never caught red-handed. Team no longer leaps as one.

(SOUND: continual bickering snarls, whip, jangling traces)

EXT. FROZEN CREEK UNDER AURORA - NIGHT

Dub flushes a snowshoe rabbit. Whole team in full cry; fifty police huskies join. Sixty strong. BUCK leads, whining eagerly, body flashing in wan moonlight. Ecstasy of the blood lust—the old wolf-cry.

(SOUND: pack baying, rabbit shriek, aurora silence underfoot)

SPITZ cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives in shoulder to shoulder. They roll. The silent wolfish circle waits. Fang clashes fang. Buck rushes as for the throat then sweeps low; teeth close on Spitz's left foreleg—crunch of breaking bone. Then the right. Spitz on three legs. Buck manoeuvres the final rush. Shoulder meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot as Spitz disappears. Buck stands over the kill—the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. CAMP - DAWN

SPITZ missing. BUCK covered with wounds. FRANCOIS points them out by firelight.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

PERRAULT
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

FRANCOIS
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells. An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Francois harnesses SOL-LEKS in lead. Buck springs upon him, drives him back, stands in his place. Francois clubs; Buck retreats but circles just beyond range, snarling. For an hour they run him about. At last Francois throws down the club. Buck trotted in laughing triumphantly and swings into the lead. Traces fastened. They dash out.

(SOUND: club thuds avoided, triumphant dog laugh-pant, sled break-out)

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Record run. BUCK gives the law; Pike is shaken for loafing; Joe punished roundly. Team recovers solidarity, leaps as one. Teek and Koona broken in with celerity. Forty miles a day for fourteen days. Thirty Mile covered in one. They top White Pass; lights of Skaguay and shipping at their feet.

(SOUND: runners hissing, whip infrequent, jingling bells)

INT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

PERRAULT and FRANCOIS throw chests; team the centre of worshipful crowd. Official orders. Francois calls Buck, throws arms around him, weeps. That is the last of them. A Scotch half-breed takes charge for the mail train back to Dawson.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - DAY TO NIGHT

Heavy toil, heavy load. BUCK takes pride like Dave and Sol-leks, maintains discipline. At night he lies by the fire; visions come—the HAIRY MAN (shorter leg, longer arm, stringy muscles, long matted hair, head slanted, ragged fire-scorched skin, stone-headed stick, much body hair, trunk inclined, catlike) squats fearful by another fire while gleaming eyes circle in the dark.

(SOUND: fire pop, distant wolf howl in dream, half-breed cook shout)

COOK
Hey, you Buck, wake up!

Dave sickens. He cries out in traces, falls, is taken out, then bites through Sol-leks's traces to reclaim his place. They harness him again. He holds till camp, then cannot rise. A revolver shot rings behind the timber. Buck knows.

(SOUND: pain cries, revolver shot, sleds churning on)

EXT. SKAGUAY MAIN STREET - DAY

Salt Water Mail arrives wrecked. BUCK (BUCK_STARVED_SKELETON: 115 lb, coat limp draggled matted blood, ribs outlined) and mates totter. Fresh Hudson Bay dogs replace them. HAL (19-20, belt bristling cartridges, Colt and knife), CHARLES (middle-aged, weak watery eyes, fierce up-twisted mustache over drooping lip), and MERCEDES (pretty, soft) buy the team cheap.

(SOUND: street bustle, chaffering voices)

EXT. SKAGUAY CAMP - DAY

Slipshod tent half-stretched, dishes unwashed. Overloaded sled. Outside dogs added—fourteen total. Food math doomed. First start: runners frozen. Hal whips; Mercedes weeps and interferes. Sled breaks out, capsizes on the turn, spills load. Dogs dash; Hal is dragged. Citizens advise half the load, twice the dogs.

(SOUND: whip cracks, Mercedes sobs, sled crash, scattered gear)

EXT. TRAIL NORTH - DAY AFTER DAY

Late starts, slovenly camps. Overfeeding then underfeeding. Outside dogs die. Dub shot. Billee axed. Family quarrels endless. Mercedes rides—one hundred and twenty pounds last straw. Dogs become perambulating skeletons. Hal clubs them into motion. Spring thaw; bottom dropping out of the trail.

(SOUND: whip, club, weeping, weak dog pants, ice groan)

EXT. WHITE RIVER MOUTH (THORNTON'S CAMP) - DAY

Dogs drop as if struck dead. JOHN THORNTON whittles an axe-handle. Hal ignores warning about rotten ice.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

BUCK refuses. Club rains. Thornton springs, hurls Hal backward.

(SOUND: club on flesh, Thornton's inarticulate animal cry, Mercedes scream)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the remaining dogs pull out onto the ice. The back end drops; gee-pole jerks; a whole section gives way. They disappear. A yawning hole remains.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. WHITE RIVER MOUTH (THORNTON'S CAMP) - DAY (SPRING TO SUMMER)

BUCK (BUCK_THORNTON_PRIME: coat silk sheen, muscles swelled, perfect condition) wins back strength. SKEET (little Irish setter) washes his wounds daily. NIG (huge black half bloodhound half deerhound, laughing eyes) is friendly. Love—genuine passionate love—for the first time. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between hands, rests his own head upon Buck's, shakes him, calling love-oaths. Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his mouth in fierce caress.

(SOUND: river run, bird song, rough affectionate growls and murmurs)

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck will not let Thornton out of sight. Even at night he creeps to the tent flap to hear him breathe. The call sounds deep in the forest, but love draws him back.

EXT. CIRCLE CITY BAR - NIGHT

"Black" Burton strikes THORNTON. BUCK leaves the floor for Burton's throat with a roar. Throat torn open. Miners' meeting discharges Buck; his reputation spreads.

(SOUND: bar roar, Buck's attack roar, crowd clubs)

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Poling-boat in bad stretch. Rope snubs too suddenly; boat flips; THORNTON flung into wild water. BUCK swims, overhauls him; Thornton grabs tail. Current too strong. Thornton clutches a rock: "Go, Buck! Go!" Buck is hauled out half-drowned, then launched again with rope on neck and shoulders. He strikes Thornton like a battering ram. They are jerked under, smashed on rocks, veer to bank. Three broken ribs. They camp till Buck heals. HANS and PETE are there.

(SOUND: roaring rapids, shouts, choking gasps, rope strain)

INT. ELDORADO SALOON, DAWSON - NIGHT

Men boast dogs. THORNTON: "Buck can start a thousand pounds." MATTHEWSON slams a bologna-sized gold-dust sack: a thousand dollars says he can't. O'Brien stakes Thornton. Outside, sixty below, runners frozen to snow. Odds three to one against.

(SOUND: saloon din, gold sack thud, cold wind outside)

EXT. DAWSON STREET OUTSIDE ELDORADO - NIGHT

BUCK in harness before the loaded sled. THORNTON kneels, whispers.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines. Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings right; crisp crackling under runners.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Left; sled pivots, runners grate free.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. Muscles writhe like live things. Sled lurches—half inch, inch, two—then steadily. Thornton runs behind. At the hundred-yard firewood pile a roar bursts. Thornton falls on his knees, head against head, cursing Buck lovingly and softly. He refuses twelve hundred for him.

(SOUND: traces jarring, runners grating, crowd gasp then roar, Thornton's loving curses)

EXT. STEWART RIVER / EASTERN WILDERNESS - DAY (SEASONS PASS)

THORNTON, HANS, PETE, BUCK and half a dozen dogs head east for the fabled Lost Cabin. Indian-fashion hunting. Summer blizzards on divides, midnight sun, swarming gnats, strawberries by glaciers. Fall weird silent lake country. Winter obliterated trails. No cabin—but a shallow placer where gold shows like yellow butter. They heap fifty-pound moose-hide sacks like firewood outside a spruce-bough lodge.

(SOUND: pan gravel, rifle shot, wind, silence of empty lakes)

EXT. PLACER VALLEY CAMP - DAY

Idle days. BUCK muses by the fire; HAIRY MAN visions intensify. The call sounds. One night a long-drawn howl. Buck dashes through woods to an open place. A long lean timber wolf—WILD BROTHER—howls nose to sky. Buck stalks, half crouching. Wolf flees; chase; at last they sniff noses, play, run side by side across the divide. Buck remembers Thornton, turns back. Wolf howls mournfully after him.

(SOUND: wolf howl, soft whines, running paws on moss)

For two days Buck never leaves camp. Then restlessness returns. He sleeps out, kills a black bear, scatters wolverines. Blood-longing grows. He is a killer, pride in every movement. Coat more glorious; he might be mistaken for a gigantic wolf.

EXT. DIVIDE AND FOREST - DAY TO NIGHT

BUCK cuts a great bull moose (arrow-wounded, savage) from a band of twenty. For four days he never leaves it—retarding, irritating, wearing it down. At last he pulls it down. Eats, sleeps, then lopes for camp, sensing calamity. Fresh trail. He finds NIG dead with arrow, a sled-dog thrashing, HANS feathered like a porcupine, PETE killed in blankets, SKEET by the pool. THORNTON's struggle fresh-written to the water's edge. No trace leads away.

(SOUND: moose bellow fading, then pregnant forest silence, distant chant)

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE CAMP - DAY

YEEHATS dance about the wreckage. BUCK (BUCK_GHOST_DOG rising) becomes a live hurricane. He rips the chief's throat, then another's. Plunges among them tearing, rending. They shoot one another in panic; flee proclaiming the Evil Spirit. Buck drags them down like deer. Wearying, he returns. Mourns by the pool. Death leaves a great void. He has killed man—the noblest game—and is unafraid except of their weapons.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant to terror screams, Buck's fearful roaring, bodies falling)

EXT. MOONLIT CLEARING AND WOLF COUNTRY - NIGHT

Full moon. From far away sharp yelps. The wolf pack pours in a silvery flood. BUCK stands motionless. Boldest leaps; Buck breaks its neck. Three more try; slashed. Whole pack surges. Buck pivots, snaps, gashes, forced back to a high gravel-bank angle. After half an hour they draw back discomfited. WILD BROTHER advances, whines; they touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf sits, points nose at moon, howls. Others join. Buck howls. He comes out; pack crowds, sniffing. Leaders yelp; wolves swing in behind. Buck runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

(SOUND: pack yelps rising to full howl chorus, then running song of the pack)

EXT. PLACER VALLEY - DAY (YEARS LATER)

YEEHATS note change in the breed—splashes of brown on head and muzzle, white rift down chest. They fear the Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack, steals from camps, slays their dogs, defies hunters. A certain valley they never enter.

(SOUND: wind in long grasses, distant mournful howl)

Each summer a great gloriously coated wolf, like yet unlike, crosses alone, muses by the yellow stream from rotted moose-hide sacks, howls once long and mournfully, then departs. In winter nights he may be seen running at the head of the pack through pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, leaping gigantic, great throat a-bellow, singing the song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

(MUSIC: lone wolf howl swelling into full pack song under aurora)

FADE OUT.

THE END

